 "prompt_b": "SYSTEM_B + '\\n\\n' + build_prompt's text after INSTRUCTION with the whole posting (RESUME:/CANDIDATE PROFILE:/JOB POSTING:/Answer:)",
 "prompt_b_system": "You are labeling job-match rows for a test set. Each row has a job posting, a candidate's resume, and the candidate's profile (what they filled in on a form). Decide what a careful career advisor would tell this candidate about applying. Today is October 2026.\n\n- Skip: something the posting states as a hard requirement rules them out. That covers work authorization (US citizens or permanent residents only; no visa sponsorship when the candidate will need it; no OPT or CPT when they are on F-1), student status (a posting for current students when the candidate has graduated, or one for graduates when they are still a student; compare the graduation year with today), a required degree level, or a required degree field they lack unless the posting accepts \"related\" or \"equivalent\", a required license they don't hold, an employment type, location or work mode their profile rules out, stated pay below their salary floor, or a skill or amount of experience the posting clearly calls required (must have, required, minimum qualifications) that the resume doesn't show.\n- Keep: nothing rules them out and they meet the posting's stated requirements. Skills given as examples (\"such as\", \"e.g.\", \"or similar\", \"one of\", \"experience with a language like\") are met by any one of them or a close equivalent. Missing preferred, bonus or nice-to-have items do not block Keep unless most of them are missing. Duties describe the work; they are not requirements.\n- Explore: worth a look but not a clean fit. Nothing rules them out, but they are about a year short on required experience, they miss most of the preferred items, a requirement is genuinely ambiguous, or the posting says too little to judge (a short teaser with no real requirements).",
 "prompt_b_system_sha256": "1d12b0d0ec70e3dcab5e7523a3477c04fc75714564b5e33b94c31f44e7cb2e0d",
 "prompt_b_system_note": "labeler_prompt_test3's opening paragraph with rubric_bullets under it, both verbatim, nothing after the bullets; the opening sentence read as the opening paragraph",
 "prompt_b_where": "Adaption's raw upload has no system column, so the rubric opens the prompt column (the top of the single user turn in chat format); score B with local_eval --framing chat",
